      "reviewScope": "abstract-only",
      "reviewLabel": "Abstract read; full methods and results not assessed.",
      "summary": "This review examined language interventions for children aged 2–18 with several neurodevelopmental conditions. Eligible studies used randomised or quasi-experimental comparisons and addressed receptive or expressive language. The authors combined 38 studies for immediate outcomes and eight for follow-up. They found modest average improvements, but also indications that publication bias inflated the estimates. Benefits varied by the kind of language outcome measured. Reporting quality was poor, and the authors described the evidence as promising but inconclusive. For adults, the useful lesson is to choose a specific language goal and monitor whether teaching helps in everyday situations. This does not establish that any particular app, matching game or amount of practice will improve language. A published correction must be examined before this summary receives editorial approval.",
