      "reviewScope": "abstract-only",
      "reviewLabel": "Abstract read; full methods and results not assessed.",
      "summary": "This meta-analysis asked whether reading instruction helps school-aged children with ADHD who have reading difficulties. It combined 18 studies, including journal articles and dissertations, involving 564 participants. The authors reported substantial reading gains in higher-quality studies, including gains on standardised assessments. They highlighted instruction addressing decoding and phonemic awareness. The practical implication is that a child with ADHD and reading difficulty needs targeted reading teaching; attention exercises alone are not a replacement. The interventions studied involved teaching programmes, not brief standalone app games. The abstract's description of intervention hours should not be converted into an individual prescription or an app-use target. Full methods, study differences and applicability to a particular child still need examination. Our activities may provide supported practice alongside teaching, but this review did not test them.",
